---
title: Build a Sandbox Adapter (Advanced)
id: sandbox-build-an-adapter
order: 15
description: "Choose what a sandboxed run leaves behind: everything, only the sandbox side, or only the conversation. Then implement the sandbox store and prove it with the conformance suite."
keywords:
  - SandboxInstanceStore
  - withSandbox persistence
  - store sandbox runs
  - sandbox conformance suite
  - defineSandboxInstanceStore
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your agent runs in a sandbox, and you have to decide what survives a server\nrestart, a second replica, or a user closing the tab. The answer is not one switch.\nA sandboxed run has two halves that persist separately:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sandbox side."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Which provider sandbox to resume for a thread, whether a run\nis still going, and the run's event log."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The conversation."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The messages your UI paints when someone reopens a thread."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can keep both, either one, or neither. This page is the sandbox side, plus the one\nplace the two meet. It is the third of the adapter walkthroughs, next to\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-chat-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-generation-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generation"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and it needs neither of their store\ncontracts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To rebuild completed workspace files after the provider sandbox is gone, use\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/portable-snapshots-configure","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep Files After Reload"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Pass the same\npersistence object that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" uses."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"decide-what-you-store","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decide what you store"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You keep"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You get"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You give up"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Everything"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"instances"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reopen a thread on any device and see the transcript, the tool cards, and a run still in flight"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store size: one run's tool output can be hundreds of kilobytes"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sandbox only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"instances"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":", and no message store"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runs survive a refresh and can be taken over; sandboxes are reused instead of rebuilt"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No transcript. A returning client sees the live remainder, not the history"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" alone"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The conversation comes back"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No sandbox reuse and no takeover. A disconnect destroys the sandbox"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Neither"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Neither middleware"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing to operate"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every run starts from a cold sandbox and dies with its socket"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Sandbox only\" is the posture to reach for when the text itself is sensitive. Run\nrecords and the instance map hold ids and timestamps, not what anyone typed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is a finer knob inside a stored transcript, because the harness's tool calls\nare stored as messages: keep them and reopening a thread rebuilds the tool cards,\nor drop them and keep the conversation alone. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/events#trim-what-you-keep","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trim what you keep"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"keep-everything","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep everything"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" owns the conversation. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" owns the sandbox. They meet at\none place: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pass the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" to both"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so one record describes the run\ninstead of two that disagree."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// Your stores and your `defineSandbox(...)` result.\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport function agentRun(input: {\n  messages: Array<{ role: 'user'; content: string }>\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  return chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages: input.messages,\n    threadId: input.threadId,\n    runId: input.runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      // Before `withSandbox`: it serializes resume-or-create for one key.\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        instances,\n        // The SAME store chat persistence uses.\n        runs: persistence.stores.runs,\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" to make the run detachable and replayable; that is\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"keep-only-the-sandbox-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep only the sandbox side"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Drop "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" and the sandbox half still works. It needs two stores of its\nown, and neither is a chat store:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":", which is a core contract, not a persistence-package one."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxInstanceStore"},{"type":"text","value":", which is the sandbox's own (see below)."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your own `RunStore`. Nothing here stores a message.\nimport { runs } from './runs'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport function agentRun(input: {\n  messages: Array<{ role: 'user'; content: string }>\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  return chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages: input.messages,\n    threadId: input.threadId,\n    runId: input.runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, { instances, runs }),\n    ],\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A returning client can find the run ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":") and tail its remainder. It\ncannot repaint what came before, because nothing stored it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"keep-only-the-conversation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep only the conversation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" alone. Leave "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"instances"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" off "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand it keeps today's simplest behavior: a fresh sandbox per run, destroyed when the\nsocket closes. Nothing to implement on the sandbox side at all."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"implement-sandboxinstancestore","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SandboxInstanceStore"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the sandbox's own contract: a map from a compound key to the provider sandbox\nthat should be resumed. Three methods, and each one has an invariant the conformance\nsuite checks."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandboxInstanceStore } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nexport const instances = defineSandboxInstanceStore({\n  // A missing key returns null. It never throws.\n  get: (key) => db.sandboxInstances.findByKey(key),\n\n  // A FULL replace, not a merge: omitted optional fields must clear the stored\n  // value, or a create-without-snapshot leaves a stale `latestSnapshotId` behind\n  // and `ensure` resumes from a snapshot that no longer describes the workspace.\n  upsert: (record) => db.sandboxInstances.replace(record),\n\n  // Deleting a key that is not there is a no-op.\n  delete: (key) => db.sandboxInstances.remove(key),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Invariant"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A missing key returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":". It does not throw."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full replace"}]},{"type":"text","value":" by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"record.key"},{"type":"text","value":". Omitted optionals clear prior values."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A missing key is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no-op"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"timestamps"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" is epoch "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"milliseconds"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One row per key is enough:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"CREATE TABLE sandbox_instances (\n  key                 TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  provider            TEXT NOT NULL,\n  provider_sandbox_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  latest_snapshot_id  TEXT,\n  thread_id           TEXT NOT NULL,\n  latest_run_id       TEXT,\n  updated_at          INTEGER NOT NULL\n);","lang":"sql"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put it in the same database as your chat tables if you like. That is your choice, not\na requirement: the sandbox never reads a chat table and chat never reads this one."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"updated_at"},{"type":"text","value":" is yours to sweep on. The library never deletes rows on a schedule, so\nold placements are garbage you collect (see "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping & Retention"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hand it to the middleware, or provide it ambiently when a platform layer owns the\nwiring:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport const middleware = [withSandbox(sandbox, { instances })]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-four-run-fields","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The four run fields"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable runs add four optional fields to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" record you already use for\nchat. Nothing outside the sandbox packages writes them, and a chat-only app can leave\nthe columns out of its schema entirely."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Written by"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dropping it breaks"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sandboxKey"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" on detach"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nothing can find the detached sandbox again, so it is never reclaimed"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" on detach, cleared on re-attach"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reaper cannot tell a run nobody watches from a live one"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancelRequested"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requestRunCancel"},{"type":"text","value":", out of band"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Stop button cannot reach a run driven by another replica"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driverEpoch"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"each host that claims the run"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover has no fence, so two hosts can drive one run"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One rule matters more than the four names: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" must treat an "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"omitted"}]},{"type":"text","value":" key and\na key carrying "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"}]},{"type":"text","value":" differently. Omitted means \"leave the column alone\".\nExplicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" means \"clear it\", which is how a re-attaching viewer clears\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":". A backend that filters "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" out of its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SET"},{"type":"text","value":" clause passes\nevery other test and then reports healthy runs as permanently detached."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { RunRecord } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// One branch of your own `update(runId, patch)`. Key presence, not a value check.\nexport function detachedSinceColumn(\n  patch: Partial<RunRecord>,\n  sets: Array<string>,\n  params: Array<unknown>,\n): void {\n  if ('detachedSince' in patch) {\n    sets.push('detached_since = ?')\n    params.push(patch.detachedSince ?? null)\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prove it rather than reading the table twice. This suite is separate from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runPersistenceConformance"},{"type":"text","value":" precisely because most apps never need it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { runDurableRunFieldsConformance } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nrunDurableRunFieldsConformance('my postgres runs', () => persistence.stores.runs)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listReclaimable"},{"type":"text","value":" on the same store is also sandbox-only and also optional. Skip it\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reapDetachedRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" feature-detects the gap, logs one line and sweeps nothing. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping & Retention"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prove-it-with-the-conformance-suite","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prove it with the conformance suite"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not hand-write these assertions. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit"},{"type":"text","value":" ships the suite\nthat pins every invariant above, including the full-replace rule that is easy to get\nwrong with an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { runSandboxInstanceStoreConformance } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit'\nimport { freshDb } from './test-db'\n\nrunSandboxInstanceStoreConformance('postgres', async () => {\n  const db = await freshDb()\n  return db.sandboxInstances\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three more suites cover the durable-run path, each aimed at one failure that is\npainful to find by hand:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runJournalConformance"},{"type":"text","value":": the agent's output survives as a file a successor can\nreplay. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/journal","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Run Journal"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runTakeoverConformance"},{"type":"text","value":": a second host adopts a detached run and delivers the\nremainder exactly once. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runReaperConformance"},{"type":"text","value":": a run nobody came back for is finalized or expired, and a\nstill-producing one is left alone. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/reaping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reaping & Retention"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to go next"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/durability","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sandbox Instance Durability"}]},{"type":"text","value":" has the wiring and the locking\nrules for the store you just built."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/events","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Events"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers what a stored transcript holds, and how to trim it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/durable-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Runs Explained"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the same subject in plain language with\nno code, if the postures above felt abrupt."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"decide-what-you-store","text":"Decide what you store","level":2},{"id":"keep-everything","text":"Keep everything","level":2},{"id":"keep-only-the-sandbox-side","text":"Keep only the sandbox side","level":2},{"id":"keep-only-the-conversation","text":"Keep only the conversation","level":2},{"id":"implement-sandboxinstancestore","text":"Implement SandboxInstanceStore","level":2},{"id":"the-four-run-fields","text":"The four run fields","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-with-the-conformance-suite","text":"Prove it with the conformance suite","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYour agent runs in a sandbox, and you have to decide what survives a server\nrestart, a second replica, or a user closing the tab. The answer is not one switch.\nA sandboxed run has two halves that persist separately:\n\n- **The sandbox side.** Which provider sandbox to resume for a thread, whether a run\n  is still going, and the run's event log.\n- **The conversation.** The messages your UI paints when someone reopens a thread.\n\nYou can keep both, either one, or neither. This page is the sandbox side, plus the one\nplace the two meet. It is the third of the adapter walkthroughs, next to\n[chat](./build-your-own-chat-adapter) and\n[generation](./build-your-own-generation-adapter), and it needs neither of their store\ncontracts.\n\nTo rebuild completed workspace files after the provider sandbox is gone, use\n[Keep Files After Reload](../sandbox/portable-snapshots-configure). Pass the same\npersistence object that `withPersistence` uses.\n\n## Decide what you store\n\n| You keep | Wire | You get | You give up |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Everything | `withPersistence` + `withSandbox` with `instances`, `runs`, `durability` | Reopen a thread on any device and see the transcript, the tool cards, and a run still in flight | Store size: one run's tool output can be hundreds of kilobytes |\n| Sandbox only | `withSandbox` with `instances`, `runs`, `durability`, and no message store | Runs survive a refresh and can be taken over; sandboxes are reused instead of rebuilt | No transcript. A returning client sees the live remainder, not the history |\n| Chat only | `withPersistence` alone | The conversation comes back | No sandbox reuse and no takeover. A disconnect destroys the sandbox |\n| Neither | Neither middleware | Nothing to operate | Every run starts from a cold sandbox and dies with its socket |\n\n\"Sandbox only\" is the posture to reach for when the text itself is sensitive. Run\nrecords and the instance map hold ids and timestamps, not what anyone typed.\n\nThere is a finer knob inside a stored transcript, because the harness's tool calls\nare stored as messages: keep them and reopening a thread rebuilds the tool cards,\nor drop them and keep the conversation alone. See\n[Trim what you keep](../sandbox/events#trim-what-you-keep).\n\n## Keep everything\n\n`withPersistence` owns the conversation. `withSandbox` owns the sandbox. They meet at\none place: **pass the same `RunStore` to both**, so one record describes the run\ninstead of two that disagree.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// Your stores and your `defineSandbox(...)` result.\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport function agentRun(input: {\n  messages: Array<{ role: 'user'; content: string }>\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  return chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages: input.messages,\n    threadId: input.threadId,\n    runId: input.runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      // Before `withSandbox`: it serializes resume-or-create for one key.\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        instances,\n        // The SAME store chat persistence uses.\n        runs: persistence.stores.runs,\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n}\n```\n\nAdd `durability` to make the run detachable and replayable; that is\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover).\n\n## Keep only the sandbox side\n\nDrop `withPersistence` and the sandbox half still works. It needs two stores of its\nown, and neither is a chat store:\n\n- a `RunStore`, which is a core contract, not a persistence-package one.\n- a `SandboxInstanceStore`, which is the sandbox's own (see below).\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your own `RunStore`. Nothing here stores a message.\nimport { runs } from './runs'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport function agentRun(input: {\n  messages: Array<{ role: 'user'; content: string }>\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  return chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages: input.messages,\n    threadId: input.threadId,\n    runId: input.runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, { instances, runs }),\n    ],\n  })\n}\n```\n\nA returning client can find the run (`findActiveRun`) and tail its remainder. It\ncannot repaint what came before, because nothing stored it.\n\n## Keep only the conversation\n\n`withPersistence` alone. Leave `instances`, `runs` and `durability` off `withSandbox`\nand it keeps today's simplest behavior: a fresh sandbox per run, destroyed when the\nsocket closes. Nothing to implement on the sandbox side at all.\n\n## Implement `SandboxInstanceStore`\n\nThis is the sandbox's own contract: a map from a compound key to the provider sandbox\nthat should be resumed. Three methods, and each one has an invariant the conformance\nsuite checks.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxInstanceStore } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nexport const instances = defineSandboxInstanceStore({\n  // A missing key returns null. It never throws.\n  get: (key) => db.sandboxInstances.findByKey(key),\n\n  // A FULL replace, not a merge: omitted optional fields must clear the stored\n  // value, or a create-without-snapshot leaves a stale `latestSnapshotId` behind\n  // and `ensure` resumes from a snapshot that no longer describes the workspace.\n  upsert: (record) => db.sandboxInstances.replace(record),\n\n  // Deleting a key that is not there is a no-op.\n  delete: (key) => db.sandboxInstances.remove(key),\n})\n```\n\n| Method | Invariant |\n| --- | --- |\n| `get` | A missing key returns `null`. It does not throw. |\n| `upsert` | **Full replace** by `record.key`. Omitted optionals clear prior values. |\n| `delete` | A missing key is a **no-op**. |\n| timestamps | `updatedAt` is epoch **milliseconds**. |\n\nOne row per key is enough:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE sandbox_instances (\n  key                 TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  provider            TEXT NOT NULL,\n  provider_sandbox_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  latest_snapshot_id  TEXT,\n  thread_id           TEXT NOT NULL,\n  latest_run_id       TEXT,\n  updated_at          INTEGER NOT NULL\n);\n```\n\nPut it in the same database as your chat tables if you like. That is your choice, not\na requirement: the sandbox never reads a chat table and chat never reads this one.\n\n`updated_at` is yours to sweep on. The library never deletes rows on a schedule, so\nold placements are garbage you collect (see [Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping)).\n\nHand it to the middleware, or provide it ambiently when a platform layer owns the\nwiring:\n\n```ts\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport const middleware = [withSandbox(sandbox, { instances })]\n```\n\n## The four run fields\n\nDurable runs add four optional fields to the `RunStore` record you already use for\nchat. Nothing outside the sandbox packages writes them, and a chat-only app can leave\nthe columns out of its schema entirely.\n\n| Field | Written by | Dropping it breaks |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `sandboxKey` | `withSandbox` on detach | Nothing can find the detached sandbox again, so it is never reclaimed |\n| `detachedSince` | `withSandbox` on detach, cleared on re-attach | The reaper cannot tell a run nobody watches from a live one |\n| `cancelRequested` | `requestRunCancel`, out of band | A Stop button cannot reach a run driven by another replica |\n| `driverEpoch` | each host that claims the run | Takeover has no fence, so two hosts can drive one run |\n\nOne rule matters more than the four names: `update` must treat an **omitted** key and\na key carrying **`undefined`** differently. Omitted means \"leave the column alone\".\nExplicit `undefined` means \"clear it\", which is how a re-attaching viewer clears\n`detachedSince`. A backend that filters `undefined` out of its `SET` clause passes\nevery other test and then reports healthy runs as permanently detached.\n\n```ts\nimport type { RunRecord } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// One branch of your own `update(runId, patch)`. Key presence, not a value check.\nexport function detachedSinceColumn(\n  patch: Partial<RunRecord>,\n  sets: Array<string>,\n  params: Array<unknown>,\n): void {\n  if ('detachedSince' in patch) {\n    sets.push('detached_since = ?')\n    params.push(patch.detachedSince ?? null)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nProve it rather than reading the table twice. This suite is separate from\n`runPersistenceConformance` precisely because most apps never need it:\n\n```ts\nimport { runDurableRunFieldsConformance } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nrunDurableRunFieldsConformance('my postgres runs', () => persistence.stores.runs)\n```\n\n`listReclaimable` on the same store is also sandbox-only and also optional. Skip it\nand `reapDetachedRuns` feature-detects the gap, logs one line and sweeps nothing. See\n[Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping).\n\n## Prove it with the conformance suite\n\nDo not hand-write these assertions. `@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit` ships the suite\nthat pins every invariant above, including the full-replace rule that is easy to get\nwrong with an `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`:\n\n```ts\nimport { runSandboxInstanceStoreConformance } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit'\nimport { freshDb } from './test-db'\n\nrunSandboxInstanceStoreConformance('postgres', async () => {\n  const db = await freshDb()\n  return db.sandboxInstances\n})\n```\n\nThree more suites cover the durable-run path, each aimed at one failure that is\npainful to find by hand:\n\n- `runJournalConformance`: the agent's output survives as a file a successor can\n  replay. See [The Run Journal](../sandbox/journal).\n- `runTakeoverConformance`: a second host adopts a detached run and delivers the\n  remainder exactly once. See [Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover).\n- `runReaperConformance`: a run nobody came back for is finalized or expired, and a\n  still-producing one is left alone. See [Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping).\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Sandbox Instance Durability](../sandbox/durability) has the wiring and the locking\n  rules for the store you just built.\n- [Events](../sandbox/events) covers what a stored transcript holds, and how to trim it.\n- [Durable Runs Explained](../sandbox/durable-runs) is the same subject in plain language with\n  no code, if the postures above felt abrupt.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Build a Sandbox Adapter (Advanced)\nid: sandbox-build-an-adapter\norder: 15\ndescription: \"Choose what a sandboxed run leaves behind: everything, only the sandbox side, or only the conversation. Then implement the sandbox store and prove it with the conformance suite.\"\nkeywords:\n  - SandboxInstanceStore\n  - withSandbox persistence\n  - store sandbox runs\n  - sandbox conformance suite\n  - defineSandboxInstanceStore\n---\n\nYour agent runs in a sandbox, and you have to decide what survives a server\nrestart, a second replica, or a user closing the tab. The answer is not one switch.\nA sandboxed run has two halves that persist separately:\n\n- **The sandbox side.** Which provider sandbox to resume for a thread, whether a run\n  is still going, and the run's event log.\n- **The conversation.** The messages your UI paints when someone reopens a thread.\n\nYou can keep both, either one, or neither. This page is the sandbox side, plus the one\nplace the two meet. It is the third of the adapter walkthroughs, next to\n[chat](./build-your-own-chat-adapter) and\n[generation](./build-your-own-generation-adapter), and it needs neither of their store\ncontracts.\n\nTo rebuild completed workspace files after the provider sandbox is gone, use\n[Keep Files After Reload](../sandbox/portable-snapshots-configure). Pass the same\npersistence object that `withPersistence` uses.\n\n## Decide what you store\n\n| You keep | Wire | You get | You give up |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Everything | `withPersistence` + `withSandbox` with `instances`, `runs`, `durability` | Reopen a thread on any device and see the transcript, the tool cards, and a run still in flight | Store size: one run's tool output can be hundreds of kilobytes |\n| Sandbox only | `withSandbox` with `instances`, `runs`, `durability`, and no message store | Runs survive a refresh and can be taken over; sandboxes are reused instead of rebuilt | No transcript. A returning client sees the live remainder, not the history |\n| Chat only | `withPersistence` alone | The conversation comes back | No sandbox reuse and no takeover. A disconnect destroys the sandbox |\n| Neither | Neither middleware | Nothing to operate | Every run starts from a cold sandbox and dies with its socket |\n\n\"Sandbox only\" is the posture to reach for when the text itself is sensitive. Run\nrecords and the instance map hold ids and timestamps, not what anyone typed.\n\nThere is a finer knob inside a stored transcript, because the harness's tool calls\nare stored as messages: keep them and reopening a thread rebuilds the tool cards,\nor drop them and keep the conversation alone. See\n[Trim what you keep](../sandbox/events#trim-what-you-keep).\n\n## Keep everything\n\n`withPersistence` owns the conversation. `withSandbox` owns the sandbox. They meet at\none place: **pass the same `RunStore` to both**, so one record describes the run\ninstead of two that disagree.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n// Your stores and your `defineSandbox(...)` result.\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport function agentRun(input: {\n  messages: Array<{ role: 'user'; content: string }>\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  return chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages: input.messages,\n    threadId: input.threadId,\n    runId: input.runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      // Before `withSandbox`: it serializes resume-or-create for one key.\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        instances,\n        // The SAME store chat persistence uses.\n        runs: persistence.stores.runs,\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n}\n```\n\nAdd `durability` to make the run detachable and replayable; that is\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover).\n\n## Keep only the sandbox side\n\nDrop `withPersistence` and the sandbox half still works. It needs two stores of its\nown, and neither is a chat store:\n\n- a `RunStore`, which is a core contract, not a persistence-package one.\n- a `SandboxInstanceStore`, which is the sandbox's own (see below).\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\n// Your own `RunStore`. Nothing here stores a message.\nimport { runs } from './runs'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport function agentRun(input: {\n  messages: Array<{ role: 'user'; content: string }>\n  threadId: string\n  runId: string\n}) {\n  return chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages: input.messages,\n    threadId: input.threadId,\n    runId: input.runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, { instances, runs }),\n    ],\n  })\n}\n```\n\nA returning client can find the run (`findActiveRun`) and tail its remainder. It\ncannot repaint what came before, because nothing stored it.\n\n## Keep only the conversation\n\n`withPersistence` alone. Leave `instances`, `runs` and `durability` off `withSandbox`\nand it keeps today's simplest behavior: a fresh sandbox per run, destroyed when the\nsocket closes. Nothing to implement on the sandbox side at all.\n\n## Implement `SandboxInstanceStore`\n\nThis is the sandbox's own contract: a map from a compound key to the provider sandbox\nthat should be resumed. Three methods, and each one has an invariant the conformance\nsuite checks.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxInstanceStore } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\nexport const instances = defineSandboxInstanceStore({\n  // A missing key returns null. It never throws.\n  get: (key) => db.sandboxInstances.findByKey(key),\n\n  // A FULL replace, not a merge: omitted optional fields must clear the stored\n  // value, or a create-without-snapshot leaves a stale `latestSnapshotId` behind\n  // and `ensure` resumes from a snapshot that no longer describes the workspace.\n  upsert: (record) => db.sandboxInstances.replace(record),\n\n  // Deleting a key that is not there is a no-op.\n  delete: (key) => db.sandboxInstances.remove(key),\n})\n```\n\n| Method | Invariant |\n| --- | --- |\n| `get` | A missing key returns `null`. It does not throw. |\n| `upsert` | **Full replace** by `record.key`. Omitted optionals clear prior values. |\n| `delete` | A missing key is a **no-op**. |\n| timestamps | `updatedAt` is epoch **milliseconds**. |\n\nOne row per key is enough:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE sandbox_instances (\n  key                 TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  provider            TEXT NOT NULL,\n  provider_sandbox_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n  latest_snapshot_id  TEXT,\n  thread_id           TEXT NOT NULL,\n  latest_run_id       TEXT,\n  updated_at          INTEGER NOT NULL\n);\n```\n\nPut it in the same database as your chat tables if you like. That is your choice, not\na requirement: the sandbox never reads a chat table and chat never reads this one.\n\n`updated_at` is yours to sweep on. The library never deletes rows on a schedule, so\nold placements are garbage you collect (see [Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping)).\n\nHand it to the middleware, or provide it ambiently when a platform layer owns the\nwiring:\n\n```ts\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { instances } from './instances'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport const middleware = [withSandbox(sandbox, { instances })]\n```\n\n## The four run fields\n\nDurable runs add four optional fields to the `RunStore` record you already use for\nchat. Nothing outside the sandbox packages writes them, and a chat-only app can leave\nthe columns out of its schema entirely.\n\n| Field | Written by | Dropping it breaks |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `sandboxKey` | `withSandbox` on detach | Nothing can find the detached sandbox again, so it is never reclaimed |\n| `detachedSince` | `withSandbox` on detach, cleared on re-attach | The reaper cannot tell a run nobody watches from a live one |\n| `cancelRequested` | `requestRunCancel`, out of band | A Stop button cannot reach a run driven by another replica |\n| `driverEpoch` | each host that claims the run | Takeover has no fence, so two hosts can drive one run |\n\nOne rule matters more than the four names: `update` must treat an **omitted** key and\na key carrying **`undefined`** differently. Omitted means \"leave the column alone\".\nExplicit `undefined` means \"clear it\", which is how a re-attaching viewer clears\n`detachedSince`. A backend that filters `undefined` out of its `SET` clause passes\nevery other test and then reports healthy runs as permanently detached.\n\n```ts\nimport type { RunRecord } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// One branch of your own `update(runId, patch)`. Key presence, not a value check.\nexport function detachedSinceColumn(\n  patch: Partial<RunRecord>,\n  sets: Array<string>,\n  params: Array<unknown>,\n): void {\n  if ('detachedSince' in patch) {\n    sets.push('detached_since = ?')\n    params.push(patch.detachedSince ?? null)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nProve it rather than reading the table twice. This suite is separate from\n`runPersistenceConformance` precisely because most apps never need it:\n\n```ts\nimport { runDurableRunFieldsConformance } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nrunDurableRunFieldsConformance('my postgres runs', () => persistence.stores.runs)\n```\n\n`listReclaimable` on the same store is also sandbox-only and also optional. Skip it\nand `reapDetachedRuns` feature-detects the gap, logs one line and sweeps nothing. See\n[Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping).\n\n## Prove it with the conformance suite\n\nDo not hand-write these assertions. `@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit` ships the suite\nthat pins every invariant above, including the full-replace rule that is easy to get\nwrong with an `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE`:\n\n```ts\nimport { runSandboxInstanceStoreConformance } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox/testkit'\nimport { freshDb } from './test-db'\n\nrunSandboxInstanceStoreConformance('postgres', async () => {\n  const db = await freshDb()\n  return db.sandboxInstances\n})\n```\n\nThree more suites cover the durable-run path, each aimed at one failure that is\npainful to find by hand:\n\n- `runJournalConformance`: the agent's output survives as a file a successor can\n  replay. See [The Run Journal](../sandbox/journal).\n- `runTakeoverConformance`: a second host adopts a detached run and delivers the\n  remainder exactly once. See [Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover).\n- `runReaperConformance`: a run nobody came back for is finalized or expired, and a\n  still-producing one is left alone. See [Reaping & Retention](../sandbox/reaping).\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [Sandbox Instance Durability](../sandbox/durability) has the wiring and the locking\n  rules for the store you just built.\n- [Events](../sandbox/events) covers what a stored transcript holds, and how to trim it.\n- [Durable Runs Explained](../sandbox/durable-runs) is the same subject in plain language with\n  no code, if the postures above felt abrupt.\n"}
